nRep2F27top.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep2F27top_rr.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep3F23tar.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep3F23top.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep3F25tar.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep3F25tar_rr.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep3F25top.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep3F25top_rr.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep3F27tar.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep3F27tar_rr.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep3F27top.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep3F27top_rr.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep4F23tar.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep4F23top.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep4F25tar.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep4F25top.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep4F25top_rr.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep4F27tar.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep4F27top.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep5F23tar.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep5F23tar_rr.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep5F23top.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep5F25tar.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep5F25tar_rr.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep5F25top.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep5F25top_rr.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep5F27tar.raw\nDHSOCSNnRep5F27top.raw\nDSSOCSNcRep1tar.raw\nDSSOCSNcRep1top.raw\nDSSOCSNcRep2top.raw\nDSSOCSNcRep3tar.raw\nDSSOCSNcRep3top.raw\nDSSOCSNcRep4tar.raw\nDSSOCSNcRep4top.raw\nDSSOCSNcRep5tar.raw\nDSSOCSNcRep5top.raw\nDSSOCSNcRep6tar.raw\nDSSOCSNcRep6top.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep1tar.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep1top.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep2top.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep3tar.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep3tar_rr.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep3top.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep3top_rr.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep4tar.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep4tar_rr.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep4top.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep4top_rr.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep5tar.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep5tar_rr.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep5top.raw\nDSSOCSNnRep5top_rr.raw\nTech1DHSOCSNcRep1.raw\nTech1DHSOCSNcRep2.raw\nTech1DHSOCSNnRep1.raw\nTech1DHSOCSNnRep2.raw\nTech2DHSOCSNcRep1.raw\nTech2DHSOCSNcRep2.raw\nTech2DHSOCSNnRep1.raw\nTech2DHSOCSNnRep2.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nIntegrative Structural Characterization of the Human Canonical and Non-canonical Cop9 Signalosome Complexes\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe COP9 signalosome (CSN) is an evolutionarily conserved protein complex that functions as a deneddylase to inactivate Cullin-RING E3 ligases for controlling protein ubiquitination. CSN possesses structural flexibility that is important for its activation upon binding to a diverse array of CRLs. The canonical and non-canonical CSN complexes consist of 8 (CSN1-8) and 9 (CSN1-9) subunits, respectively. Although CSN9 is not essential for CSN assembly and function, it appears to be important in non-catalytic regulation of CRLs by CSN. Here we employed a combinatory cross-linking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) approach to generate the largest PPI maps of human CSN complexes, which significantly enhanced the precision of integrative structural modeling. The resulting integrative structures allowed us not only to elucidate architectures of both complexes, but also to assess CSN structural dynamics that was not described in the crystal structure. In addition, we have determined CSN9 docking sites and its impact on the CSN structure. While CSN9 binding did not induce global conformational changes, it triggered subunit local reorientations that may be associated with CSN9 involvement in CSN-mediated steric regulation of CRLs.\n\n[METHODS]\nExpression and Purification of CSN complexes  Eight of total nine subunits of human COP9 signalosome (CSN) complex, except CSN5, were over-expressed and purified from E. coli. Two three-subunit subcomplexes, CSN1-2-3 and CSN4-6-7, were prepared through co-expression. Briefly, CSN2 was subcloned into a modified pGEX4T1 (Amersham Biosciences) vector containing a glutathione S-transferase (GST) tag followed by a Tobacco etch virus (TEV) protease cleavage site, while both CSN1 and CSN3 were subcloned into a modified pET15b (Novagen) vector containing a chloramphenicol resistance cassette. After co-expression in BL21(DE3) (Novagen), the CSN1-2-3 formed a complex and was purified by glutathione-affinity chromatography. Following TEV cleavage, the CSN1-2-3 subcomplex was further purified by anion exchange and gel filtration chromatography. CSN4-6-7 was prepared in the same way. CSN8 and CSN9 were subcloned into the pGEX4T1 vector individually and subjected to the same purification procedure. Recombinant full length CSN5 was prepared from insect cells using baculovirus expression system. Two CSN complexes, with or without CSN9, were reconstituted by incubating the purified subcomplexes and individual subunits in equimolar ratio and polished by size exclusion chromatography. Neddylated Cul1-Rbx1 complex was prepared as described previously {Yu, 2015}.  Chemical Cross-linking of CSN Complexes Affinity purified human CSN complex with or without CSN9 were cross-linked with DSSO, DHSO, or BMSO, respectively. Each CSN complex was reacted with a selected cross-linker at their optimized molar ratios (protein to linker) respectively: DSSO (1:250), BMSO (1:400) and DHSO (1:30) {Kao, 2011; Gutierrez, 2016; Gutierrez, 2018}. DMTMM was used to activate acidic residues for DHSO cross-linking {Gutierrez, 2016}. All reactions were performed for 1 h at room temperature. The resulting cross-linked proteins were digested by lys-C and trypsin, and the resulting peptides were separated by peptide SEC to enrich cross-linked peptides for MS analysis as previously described {Gutierrez, 2016; Gutierrez, 2018}.  LC MSn Analysis of Cross-linked Peptides LC MSn analysis was performed using a Thermo Scientific™ Dionex UltiMate 3000 system online coupled with an Orbitrap Fusion Lumos™ mass spectrometer. A 50 cm x 75 μm Acclaim™ PepMap™ C18 column was used to separate peptides over a gradient of 1% to 25% ACN in 114 mins at a flow rate of 300 nL/min. Two different types of acquisition methods were utilized to maximize the identification of DSSO, DHSO and BMSO cross-linked peptides: (1) top 4 data-dependent MS3 and (2) targeted MS3 acquisition optimized for capturing DSSO, DHSO and BMSO cross-linked peptides by utilizing the mass difference between characteristic MS2 fragment ions of DSSO cross-linked peptides (\uf0e1−β) (i.e. Δ = \uf0e1T−\uf0e1A = βT−βA = 31.9721 Da) {Yu, 2016}.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
